## Important note

The `update_aggregate` function above works, but the initialization/update logic is more awkward than it should be for a clean first version.

Here is a cleaner replacement for `update_aggregate()` that you should use instead:

```rust
fn update_aggregate(state: &AppState, trade: Trade) -> Aggregate {
    let symbol = trade.symbol.clone();

    let mut entry = state
        .aggregates
        .entry(symbol.clone())
        .or_insert_with(|| AggregateState::new(&trade));

    if entry.trade_count != 1
        || entry.last_ts_ms != trade.ts_ms
        || entry.last_price != trade.price
        || entry.volume != trade.size
    {
        if !(entry.trade_count == 1
            && entry.last_ts_ms == trade.ts_ms
            && entry.last_price == trade.price
            && entry.volume == trade.size)
        {
            entry.update(&trade);
        }
    }

    entry.snapshot(&symbol)
}
```

And even better, the best version is this one:

```rust
fn update_aggregate(state: &AppState, trade: Trade) -> Aggregate {
    let symbol = trade.symbol.clone();

    use dashmap::mapref::entry::Entry;
    match state.aggregates.entry(symbol.clone()) {
        Entry::Occupied(mut occ) => {
            occ.get_mut().update(&trade);
            occ.get().snapshot(&symbol)
        }
        Entry::Vacant(vac) => {
            let agg = AggregateState::new(&trade);
            let snapshot = agg.snapshot(&symbol);
            vac.insert(agg);
            snapshot
        }
    }
}
```

Use that final version.

