## Why this is memory-constrained friendly

- no dynamic memory allocation in steady state
- only fixed-size stack buffers
- polling loop is simple and bounded
- no threads
- no runtime GC
- no heavyweight libraries

---

## Important operational note

This daemon rotates by renaming the current log file and creating a new one.  
That works best when the writer:

- reopens the log file on `SIGHUP`, or
- opens/closes on each write, or
- writes by pathname rather than keeping the old fd forever

If the writer keeps the old file descriptor open indefinitely, it may continue writing into the renamed `.1` file after rotation. That is standard Unix log rotation behavior.

