# How rotation works

If `/tmp/app.log` grows too large:

Before:
```text
/tmp/app.log
/tmp/app.log.1
/tmp/app.log.2
```

After rotation:
```text
/tmp/app.log      <- recreated empty
/tmp/app.log.1    <- previous /tmp/app.log
/tmp/app.log.2    <- previous /tmp/app.log.1
/tmp/app.log.3    <- previous /tmp/app.log.2
```

---

# Memory characteristics

This implementation is intentionally small:
- no heap allocation in steady state
- fixed stack buffers only
- no parsing libraries
- no threads
- no event framework
- no large buffers

The biggest memory consumers are just:
- process/runtime overhead
- a few stack buffers (`PATH_MAX`-sized)
- libc/system call state

---

# Important operational note

This is a **minimal demo**, not a production-complete rotator.

A real production daemon often also needs:
- PID file locking
- prevention of multiple instances
- configurable ownership/permissions
- copytruncate mode for writers that keep file handles open
- signal/reopen integration
- compression of old logs
- syslog/journald error reporting
- handling of cross-filesystem rotation
- race-condition hardening
- config file support

---

# Biggest limitation of this minimal version

It uses `rename(path, path.1)` and recreates the file.

That works well when:
- the logging application reopens the log file, or
- the application writes by path each time

But if the application keeps the old file descriptor open, it may continue writing to the renamed file instead of the new active log.

In that case you usually want one of:
- `copytruncate` style rotation
- sending the writer a signal to reopen logs
- integrating directly with the writer

