## How rotation works

If `/tmp/app.log` exceeds the limit:

- `/tmp/app.log.5` is removed
- `/tmp/app.log.4` becomes `/tmp/app.log.5`
- `/tmp/app.log.3` becomes `/tmp/app.log.4`
- ...
- `/tmp/app.log.1` becomes `/tmp/app.log.2`
- `/tmp/app.log` becomes `/tmp/app.log.1`
- a new empty `/tmp/app.log` is created

---

## Important limitation

This is intentionally minimal. If another process is actively writing to the log file and keeps the file descriptor open, simple renaming may not fully behave like production log rotation systems unless the writer reopens the file after rotation.

In real deployments, common approaches are:

- send the writer a signal to reopen logs
- use `copytruncate`-style logic
- integrate directly with the logging process

---

## Why this is memory-constrained

This implementation is suitable for strict memory budgets because it:

- uses fixed-size stack buffers only
- does not allocate on the heap
- avoids large libraries/frameworks
- does not maintain in-memory state beyond a few variables
- performs only simple `stat`, `rename`, `unlink`, and `open` operations

