## Memory characteristics

Why this is memory-constrained-friendly:

- No malloc/free
- Fixed-size stack buffers only
- No threads
- No large in-memory state
- No parsing of large config files
- No external runtime or VM
- Polling loop with constant memory use

---

## Important limitations

This is minimal, so it does not include several things a production daemon often needs:

- No PID file
- No syslog/journald logging
- No config reload on SIGHUP
- No compression of old logs
- No copytruncate mode
- No file locking
- No handling for applications that keep the old file descriptor open after rename
- No inotify/kqueue-based event watching
- No privilege dropping
- No seccomp/caps/chroot hardening
- No robust race handling with multiple writers/rotators

One especially important limitation:

If the application writing the log keeps the file open, simple `rename()` rotation may not be enough. The writer may continue writing to the renamed file instead of the newly created one unless the writer reopens the log on signal or supports log reopening.

