## Why this is a good minimal baseline

It shows the essential daemon patterns:

- background execution
- singleton via PID file
- periodic monitoring loop
- shared mutable state
- local command/control interface
- graceful shutdown

---

## Important production improvements

For a real high-performance production daemon, I would strongly recommend adding:

- `systemd` integration instead of manual daemonization on modern Linux
- proper structured logging
- signal handling for `SIGTERM`/`SIGHUP`
- privilege dropping
- socket file permissions and authentication
- resource limits
- metrics endpoint
- epoll or async I/O instead of thread-per-client
- cgroup/process management actions
- safer PID file locking
- config file support
- unit tests and integration tests

---

## Optional: systemd service file

If you want to run it under `systemd`, use this instead of self-daemonizing.

### `/etc/systemd/system/sysmon_daemon.service`
```ini
[Unit]
Description=Minimal System Resource Monitoring Daemon
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/path/to/target/release/sysmon_daemon
Restart=always
User=root

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Then:
```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable sysmon_daemon
sudo systemctl start sysmon_daemon
```

