## Optional: smaller alternative filter

If memory is extremely tight, an **exponential moving average** uses less RAM than a moving average ring buffer.

Example:

```c
typedef struct {
    uint16_t value;
    bool initialized;
} EmaFilter;

static void ema_init(EmaFilter *f)
{
    f->value = 0;
    f->initialized = false;
}

/* alpha = 1/4 implemented with shifts */
static uint16_t ema_update(EmaFilter *f, uint16_t sample)
{
    if (!f->initialized) {
        f->value = sample;
        f->initialized = true;
    } else {
        f->value = (uint16_t)((3U * f->value + sample) / 4U);
    }
    return f->value;
}
```

This is often better for very small systems.

